From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rocket: wait for a running IRQ handler before resetting a
 core

rocket_reset() calls drm_sched_stop(), which stops the scheduler and
returns.  It does not wait for a threaded handler that is already
running, so the comment that followed it -- "Remaining interrupts have
been handled" -- stated an assumption rather than something the code
arranged.  A completion handler that is mid-flight when the reset starts
keeps running against a core the reset is about to put through
rocket_core_reset(), and against an in_flight_job the reset has already
dropped.

Call synchronize_irq(core->irq) after drm_sched_stop() and reword the
comment to say what holds afterwards.

Mask the block first.  rocket_job_hw_submit() arms a completion pair in
INTERRUPT_MASK on every submit and only the hardirq clears it, so on an
ordinary timeout the mask is still live and a completion can arrive
after the sync returns.  Waiting without masking fences the handler that
is running, not the one the hardware is still entitled to raise.  The
next submit re-arms the mask, so nothing is lost by clearing it here.

Clear the raw status alongside it.  Masking alone leaves the completion
bit latched until rocket_core_reset(), and rocket_job_irq_handler()
decides on INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS alone -- it reads the raw register and
wakes the thread on core->irq_done_mask, never consulting the mask it
just cleared.  The line is IRQF_SHARED, so an interrupt from anything
else on it re-enters the hardirq, finds the stale bit and wakes the
threaded handler again after the sync below has already returned.

That write is guarded by pm_runtime_get_if_active(), and it needs to be.
It is the first register access rocket_reset() makes, and the function
holds no runtime PM reference of its own: the only one in the window
belongs to in_flight_job, and the completion path can have put it and
cleared the pointer before the timeout worker arrives.  drm_sched_stop()
sits in between, can block, and subtracts every pending job's credits,
so rocket_job_is_idle() is true and nothing keeps the core resumed.  A
register access with the domain down takes an async SError on this
hardware, so the reset must not be what causes one.

Only a positive answer will do, and > 0 is not shorthand for != 0.
pm_runtime_get_if_active() tests power.disable_depth before
power.runtime_status, so -EINVAL masks a suspended device rather than
excluding one, and this driver makes that state twice:
pm_runtime_force_suspend(), its own system sleep callback, disables
runtime PM before it turns the clocks off, and rocket_core_fini()
suspends the core and disables before it cancels the timeout worker.
Either would put these writes on a powered-down block.  What that costs
is the other half of the ambiguity: a core still up with runtime PM
disabled -- pm_runtime_force_suspend() before its callback has run, or
CONFIG_PM=n under COMPILE_TEST -- keeps its mask and its latched bits,
because writing to it would mean writing to the half that is down as
well.

Those two writes go under job_lock, though the sync does not.
rocket_job_hw_submit() arms the same register and always runs under that
lock, while reset.pending is set here without it and read there with it,
so a submit that has already passed its check can re-arm the mask after
this clears it.  The block is then left running a task with its interrupt
live while synchronize_irq() fences a handler that has already finished,
which is the same shape of race 090 closes for OPERATION_ENABLE, on the
other register.  It takes a multi-task job to reach: only then does
rocket_job_hw_submit() run from the IRQ thread rather than the scheduler
thread, where drm_sched_stop() does not fence it.
pm_runtime_get_if_active() takes a reference on an already-active device
without invoking a callback, and pm_runtime_put_autosuspend() is
asynchronous, so holding job_lock across them cannot re-enter this
driver's runtime PM callbacks.

The sync itself stays outside job_lock.  rocket_job_handle_irq() takes
that lock -- 090 moved the register writes under it, so it now holds the
lock for the whole handler -- and waiting for the handler while holding
the lock it wants would deadlock instead of fencing anything.  Nothing is
held at that point, and both callers, rocket_job_timedout() and
rocket_reset_work(), run in process context, so sleeping there is
allowed.

This does not stop a handler that has already read in_flight_job from
finishing its work on the job the reset is about to drop.  That window
needs the check and the register writes to be one step under the lock,
which is what 090 does; the two are complementary.

From the v14 posting of the RK3576 series -- Jiaxing Hu's patch, suggested
by Igor Paunovic -- carried here because the function is the RK3588's own
and the fix is not RK3576-specific.

  https://lore.kernel.org/linux-rockchip/20260924102135.92217-4-gahing@gahingwoo.com/

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c | 73 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 70 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index 00611d31e..e4049499a 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -478,9 +478,76 @@ rocket_reset(struct rocket_core *core, struct drm_sched_job *bad)
 	drm_sched_stop(&core->sched, bad);
 
 	/*
-	 * Remaining interrupts have been handled, but we might still have
-	 * stuck jobs. Let's make sure the PM counters stay balanced by
-	 * manually calling pm_runtime_put_noidle().
+	 * Mask the block before waiting.  rocket_job_hw_submit() arms a
+	 * completion pair in INTERRUPT_MASK on every submit and only the
+	 * hardirq clears it, so on an ordinary timeout it is still live and a
+	 * completion can arrive after the sync below returns.  The next submit
+	 * re-arms it, so nothing is lost here.
+	 *
+	 * Only when the device is already awake, though.  This function holds
+	 * no runtime PM reference of its own: the only one in the window
+	 * belongs to in_flight_job, and the completion path may have put it
+	 * and cleared the pointer before the timeout worker got here.
+	 * drm_sched_stop() above can block for a long time, and it drops
+	 * every pending job's credits, so rocket_job_is_idle() is true and
+	 * nothing keeps the core resumed.  On this hardware a register access
+	 * with the domain down takes an async SError, so a reset must not be
+	 * the thing that causes one.
+	 *
+	 * Only a positive answer will do.  pm_runtime_get_if_active() tests
+	 * power.disable_depth before power.runtime_status, so -EINVAL MASKS a
+	 * suspended device rather than excluding one, and this driver makes
+	 * that state in both pm_runtime_force_suspend() and
+	 * rocket_core_fini().
+	 *
+	 * The cost is the other half of that ambiguity.  A core that is still
+	 * up with runtime PM disabled -- pm_runtime_force_suspend() before its
+	 * callback has run, or CONFIG_PM=n under COMPILE_TEST -- keeps its mask
+	 * and its latched bits, because writing to it would mean writing to the
+	 * half that is down as well.
+	 *
+	 * Clear the raw status along with the mask.  Masking alone leaves the
+	 * completion bit latched until rocket_core_reset(), and the hardirq
+	 * decides on INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS alone, so on this shared line
+	 * another device's interrupt would wake the thread again and what the
+	 * comment below asserts would stop holding.
+	 *
+	 * Under job_lock, because rocket_job_hw_submit() arms this same
+	 * register and always runs under that lock.  reset.pending is set in
+	 * rocket_job_timedout() without the lock and read in hw_submit() with
+	 * it, so a submit that has already passed its check can re-arm the mask
+	 * after this clears it: the block is then left running a task with its
+	 * interrupt live, while the sync below fences a handler that has
+	 * already finished.  That is the race 090 closed on OPERATION_ENABLE,
+	 * on the other register.
+	 *
+	 * pm_runtime_get_if_active() takes a reference on an already-active
+	 * device without invoking a callback, and pm_runtime_put_autosuspend()
+	 * is asynchronous, so holding job_lock across them cannot re-enter this
+	 * driver's runtime PM callbacks.
+	 */
+	scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) {
+		if (pm_runtime_get_if_active(core->dev) > 0) {
+			rocket_pc_writel(core, INTERRUPT_MASK, 0x0);
+			rocket_pc_writel(core, INTERRUPT_CLEAR, 0x1ffff);
+			pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(core->dev);
+		}
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * drm_sched_stop() returns without waiting for a threaded handler that
+	 * is already running, so wait for one here.  This has to stay outside
+	 * job_lock: rocket_job_handle_irq() takes that lock, so waiting for the
+	 * handler while holding it would be waiting for a handler that is
+	 * waiting for us.  Nothing is held at this point, and both callers run
+	 * in process context, so sleeping here is allowed.
+	 */
+	synchronize_irq(core->irq);
+
+	/*
+	 * No handler is running now, but we might still have stuck jobs. Let's
+	 * make sure the PM counters stay balanced by manually calling
+	 * pm_runtime_put_noidle().
 	 */
 	scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) {
 		if (core->in_flight_job)
-- 
